`
			},
			checks: [
				{ id: 'model-exists', kind: 'exists', selector: '#cms-model' },
				{ id: 'no-blanks', kind: 'text', selector: '#cms-model', matches: '^[^…]*$' },
				{ id: 'types', kind: 'count', selector: '#model tr.type', min: 4 },
				{ id: 'fields-listed', kind: 'text', selector: '#model td.fields', matches: ',' },
				{ id: 'owner-named', kind: 'text', selector: '#model td.owner', matches: '[Oo]wner|[Ee]ditor|[Dd]eveloper' },
				{ id: 'freq-named', kind: 'text', selector: '#model td.freq', matches: '[Oo]nce|[Ee]very|[Dd]aily|[Rr]are|[Oo]ften|[Ff]requent|[Ww]eek|[Mm]onth|[Yy]ear|[Qq]uarter|[Hh]alf' },
				{ id: 'section-per-row', kind: 'count', selector: '#sections-map tr.sec', min: { brief: 'sections' } },
				{ id: 'sections-named', kind: 'field', selector: '#sections-map tr.sec', field: 'sections' },
				{
					id: 'multilingual',
					kind: 'text',
					selector: '#model td.fields',
					matches: '_en|_ru|_es|_fr|_de|each language|every language|translat|\\([a-z]{2}\\)',
					when: { brief: 'languages', contains: 'ru' }
				}
			]
		},
		quiz: [
			{
				type: 'match',
				prompt: 'Match each layer of a site to what it holds',
				pairs: [
					['Content', 'text, prices, photos, names'],
					['Template', 'the shared header and footer, and how a service card looks'],
					['Code', 'what the site does: show a page, take a request, check access']
				]
			},
			{
				type: 'single',
				prompt: 'What makes a CMS site easier than a site of HTML files for someone who changes prices?',
				options: [
					'They edit the content in an admin screen and need neither HTML nor uploading files',
					'A CMS site needs no hosting',
					'A CMS site has no templates'
				],
				answer: 0,
				why: [
					'Yes. A CMS separates content from design and code, so an editor needs only a form in the admin screen.',
					'No: a CMS needs a server and a database, like any dynamic site.',
					'No: a CMS has templates, and the shared header lives in just one place.'
				]
			},
			{
				type: 'single',
				prompt: 'What does “model the content, not the pages” mean?',
				options: [
					'Describe the kinds of “things” (a service, a person, a review) and their fields, and build the pages from them',
					'Draw a mock-up of every page',
					'Write all the texts in advance'
				],
				answer: 0,
				why: [
					'Yes. A service is described once, and the card, the page and the list all show its fields. Change a field and everything changes.',
					'No: a mock-up is design, and a model describes content.',
					'No: a model is about how the data is arranged, not about finished texts.'
				]
			},
			{
				type: 'multiple',
				prompt: 'What does a CMS usually include?',
				options: [
					'An admin screen for editing content',
					'Storage for the content (a database or files)',
					'Roles: who can change what',
					'Ready-made text for your site'
				],
				answer: [0, 1, 2],
				why: [
					'Yes: the admin screen is the very reason people choose a CMS.',
					'Yes: the content has to live somewhere, most often in a database.',
					'Yes: an owner, an editor and a developer see different things in the admin screen.',
					'No: you will have to write the text yourself.'
				]
			},
			{
				type: 'single',
				prompt: 'A site in English and Spanish. What is the best way to arrange the “service name” field?',
				options: [
					'A field for each language (title_en, title_es), or a translation of one record linked to the original',
					'One field holding both names with a slash between them',
					'Two separate sites with no link between them'
				],
				answer: 0,
				why: [
					'Yes. That shows it is the same object in different languages, and the template picks the right one.',
					'No: you cannot pull them apart later, and the template cannot show just one name.',
					'No: the link is lost, and you would have to change the price in two places.'
				]
			},
			{
				type: 'order',
				prompt: 'Put the steps of building a content model in order',
				items: [
					'List the “things” the site shows: services, people, proofs',
					'For each, decide what fields it has and of what kind',
					'Assign who edits it and how often',
					'Decide which types each section of the site shows'
				]
			}
		],
		cards: [
			{
				id: 'cms-what',
				front: 'What is a CMS?',
				back: 'A content management system: an admin screen, storage (usually a database), templates, roles and publishing. It separates content from design and code, so an editor does not need a developer.'
			},
			{
				id: 'cms-layers',
				front: 'The three layers of a site',
				back: 'Content (text, prices, photos), template (header, footer, how a card looks) and code (what the site does). In a site of files they are mixed together; in a CMS they are separate.'
			},
			{
				id: 'cms-model-def',
				front: 'What is a content model?',
				back: 'A description of the types of content (a service, a person, a review), their fields and their links. You model the content, not the pages: pages are built from records.'
			},
			{
				id: 'cms-model-lang',
				front: 'How do you store text in several languages?',
				back: 'A field for each language (title_en, title_es), or a translation of the record linked to the original. That shows it is one object, and a price or a photo is not changed twice.'
			},
			{
				id: 'cms-who-edits',
				front: 'Which two questions decide the choice of a CMS?',
				back: 'Who edits the content (the owner, an editor, a developer) and how often. The answers show whether you need a friendly admin screen or files are enough.'
			}
		],
		sources: [
			{ title: 'MDN: Glossary, CMS', url: 'https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Glossary/CMS', licence: 'CC-BY-SA', kind: 'docs', lang: 'en' },
			{ title: 'Content management system (Wikipedia)', url: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Content_management_system', licence: 'CC-BY-SA', kind: 'article', lang: 'en' },
			{ title: 'HTTP Archive Web Almanac 2024: CMS', url: 'https://almanac.httparchive.org/en/2024/cms', licence: 'CC-BY-SA', kind: 'article', lang: 'en' }
		],
		videos: [],
		guide: {
			intro: 'We’re describing the content of your site: what “things” it is made of, and who edits them. The site itself does not change yet. This is a plan.',
			hints: [
				'A type is a kind of “thing” with many instances: a service, a person, a proof. Fields are what it has: a name, text, a price, a photo.',
				'Three words are enough for “who edits”: owner, editor, developer. For “how often”: once a week, once a month, once a year.',
				'In the second table, write for each section which types it shows: a “Services” section shows services, an “About me” section shows a person.'
			],
			mistakes: {
				'model-exists': 'The model block is gone. It was in the page, so put it back.',
				'no-blanks': 'There is still an ellipsis “…” somewhere. Replace it with your own text.',
				types: 'The table needs at least four types (four rows).',
				'fields-listed': 'Write the fields separated by commas: for example, title, text, price.',
				'owner-named': 'In the “Who edits” column write: owner, editor or developer.',
				'freq-named': 'In the “How often” column write a frequency: for example, “once a month”.',
